solstone-linux Rust release install notes

This package contains the solstone app for linux, its window and canonical icons.
Open solstone from the app menu. If the app isn't running, choose
`start the solstone app` when you're ready; this sets it up to run now and when
you sign in. Opening or closing the window does not start or stop intake.
Packages do not run service installation scripts. Service setup happens only
when you choose it in the window or run `solstone-linux install-service`.

Runtime requirements:

- glibc 2.35 or newer
- GTK 4.6 or newer for the window; the app itself runs without GTK, and a computer
  with no desktop can run it terminal-only
- tmux, if you turn on terminal capture
- libpulse and a PulseAudio-compatible server (PipeWire Pulse is supported)
- GStreamer 1.0 core, base, good, PipeWire, and X11 plugins
- PipeWire and xdg-desktop-portal with ScreenCast support for Wayland screen frames
- GIO tools for opening your journal in the browser
- xdg-utils for desktop integration
- a desktop notification service; a StatusNotifier host is what shows the panel icon.
  KDE has one. On GNOME, run `solstone-linux panel-icon` after installing this package

Typical package families:

- Debian/Ubuntu: libgtk-4-1, libglib2.0-bin, libpulse0, libgstreamer1.0-0,
  gstreamer1.0-plugins-base, gstreamer1.0-plugins-good,
  gstreamer1.0-pipewire, gstreamer1.0-x, pipewire-pulse,
  xdg-desktop-portal, xdg-utils
- Fedora/RHEL: gtk4, glib2, pulseaudio-libs, gstreamer1,
  gstreamer1-plugins-base, gstreamer1-plugins-good, pipewire-gstreamer,
  pipewire-pulseaudio, xdg-desktop-portal, xdg-utils
- openSUSE: libgtk-4-1, glib2-tools, libpulse0, gstreamer, gstreamer-plugins-base,
  gstreamer-plugins-good, gstreamer-plugin-pipewire, pipewire-pulseaudio,
  xdg-desktop-portal, xdg-utils
- Arch: gtk4, glib2, libpulse, gstreamer, gst-plugins-base, gst-plugins-good,
  gst-plugin-pipewire, pipewire-pulse, xdg-desktop-portal, xdg-utils

Create a pairing link for this device in your journal, paste it in the window's
`journal` tab, then compare your journal's mark before confirming it.
The window also has status, pause and resume, settings, and recent logs you can
preview and save. `open your journal` opens your browser. Saved settings remain
in config.json, including hand-added keys; the window says when a restart is
needed. The portable archive needs the runtime packages installed separately.

To pair from a terminal, save the link as `pairing-link.txt`, then:

```bash
solstone-linux install-service
systemctl --user stop solstone-linux
solstone-linux setup < pairing-link.txt
systemctl --user start solstone-linux
```

Pairing is the only setup path, including when your journal is on this machine.
There is no URL, key, local installation of the journal or Python, or direct
fallback to configure. Setup asks you to confirm your journal's mark, or takes `--mark` when
there is no terminal. Nothing goes into your journal until you confirm.
`solstone-linux confirm` confirms a mark while the solstone app is running.
Setup still needs the app stopped, because setup takes the private-state lock.

Setup and runtime share one private-state lock. If the app is running, setup exits before consuming input and leaves intake, config, and private state unchanged. Use the same stop, setup, start sequence when an upgrade requires a new pairing link.
